A||||10||Ozzy||'88 front lower ball joint problems
Z||000000||Ozzy||08-14-99||10:08 PM||||My '88 coupe has been a little unpredictable at highway speeds. The steering is all tight so today I finally got the front up on some jackstands and began to look for loose and worn stuff. As I grabbed the top and bottom of the left wheel moving it left to right laterally it seemed fine...when I rocked it in an up and down motion I could see about 1/4" movement in the lower ball joint. The movement is right at the fixture point on the control arm so I'm not sure whats up yet.<p>My useless manual says that the ball joint is welded to the control arm but the addendum at the end seems to indicate that the '88 models allow the lower ball joint to be removed. Any input is appreciated. I know this must be helping to create the 'darting' and shudering at higher speeds.<p>Ozzy
Z||000001||Pretender||08-15-99||12:40 AM||||I just replaced the left front lower ball joint on my 88gt, it is very easy to do!...the guys on here helped me out alot, all you need to do is to go to "AUTOZONE" and rent one of their ball joint presses for free!, it requires a $100 deposit incase you plan on not returning it or something, it works like a giant C-clamp and pushes out the old or in my case broken ball joints in a matter of minutes. if you have any other questions feel free to ask!....<p>PS: I know for sure on the 88's it's not welded at all(my chiltons manual is also useless).
Z||000002||mwbackus||08-15-99||02:49 AM||||You also need NOT disconnect the tie rod ends in order to do this.  Both my Chiltons and the Hayes manual state that this is required.  It may save the cost of an alignment, but in most cases you will need one anyway.  It helps alot to remove the brake calipers so it may be a real good time to throw on a new set of pads, etc.<p>Mike
Z||000003||Ozzy||08-15-99||12:21 PM||||Thnx guys...I'm going to try my luck today at finding a replacement on the shelf at an auto parts store. My Haynes manual shows how to rig a device to remove either the top or bottom ball joint but not for installing the new one.<p>Whutz up with these damn shop manuals anyway?? I'm thinking of buying one of the Pontiac service manuals from the Fiero store but at $114.00 it better not leave ANYTHING out. In fact it had better well know how to wrench!<p>Ozzy
Z||000004||batboy||08-15-99||12:40 PM||||The top ball joints are fairly straight forward (they just bolt in), but the lower ones are trickier. If you used a ball joint press that some parts stores loan or rent out, the job is much easier. You might have to knock a spot weld or two off the old lower ball joint at the control arm too. Don't forget to chain the front springs up real good when they're compressed before you start.
Z||000005||mwbackus||08-15-99||01:58 PM||||Ozzy, I believe the diagram you are referring to is for the tool used to separate the ball joints from the knuckle.  This tool is really only needed if you intend on reuse of the ball joint.  A standard fork which can also be rented at autozone for a deposit will work just fine on a ball joint you intend to replace.  BTW, the lower joints have NO welds on the 88's.  You have two options for removal/install of the BJ.  You can rent the press from autozone, or you can remove the lower control arms completly and take them to a machine shop where they can press in/out the BJ's.  You may want to check with autozone for the ball joints too, they stock a standard quality ball joint that cost me $32.99 each.  Thats about half the cost of other sources I found.  If you decide to do this yourself, you will need some jackstands and a good floor jack(very important).  Unless you intend to remove the control arm, you need not worry much about the spring poping out, since, the shock is still in place and this would be impossible.<p>Mike
Z||000006||Pretender||08-15-99||04:59 PM||||DO NOT REMOVE THE WHOLE "LOWER CONTROLARM"!, it's a real pain in the ass. the press is the way to go, trust me.
Z||000007||Formula||08-15-99||06:54 PM||||I went to monroe to get an estimate on this and they told me that you have to replace the control arm on an 88????? 
Z||000008||Ozzy||08-15-99||07:14 PM||||Hey Humans! <p>Well...after further investigation, here's what I've come up with; First I started calling around the local parts stores and the only ones who had a suitable part on the shelf were Napa. The brand they stock (forgot the name) is $82.00 for a lower ball joint replacement. From the competition I was quoted anywhere between $72.00 (MOOG) to $30.00 for the No Name Brand! I see on the Fiero Store site replacements for $57.00. <p>I decided after looking at the condition of all of the bushings and the other side LBJ which also has some excess play in it, that I would be better of to buy all of the parts and do the whole lot at the same time and then forget about the front end. Tie rod ends and stabilizer bar bushings seem fine and the rack and pinion was replaced just before I bought the car. The shocks look like factory so are also probably ready for replacement. (Brakes have been done in the last 12 mths also)<p>As I will be obviously dismantling the front end, should I consider a spring upgrade?? The stock springs look OK and dont appear to sag too much but... Are the ball joints from the Fiero store good quality?? Someone told me Moog is the only stuff worth getting. Also the Fiero store has neophrene bushing replacement packages for the '88. ($67.00 per side I think) Have any of you installed these and found a noticable difference. Even though the UBJ's appear to be in good shape should I swap these out also?? What shocks are good value for money and work well in the Fiero? Now that you mention it mwbackus, the tool would only be good for seperating the joints. But I guess if I am going to remove the lower control arms anyhow... I don't think there is an Autozone around here but I will check it out. The tool you guys talk of would save me a trip to the machine shop. I do have a decent floor jack and jack stands. I'm begining to collect a larger inventory of tools the more I get at this car.<p>Thnx for your advice...<p>Ozzy
Z||000009||batboy||08-15-99||09:48 PM||||Moog is one of the better ball joints for the Fiero right now, but you should be able to order them a lot cheaper than what you are getting quoted locally. Even if you take the control arms off, be careful how you press the old ball joint off and the new one back on, incorrectly done it can warp the control arm. If you remove the control arms, definitely replace the bushings too.
Z||000010||Pretender||08-15-99||11:03 PM||||well i'd have to say that Monroe is full of it and thats another example of the frachise associated mechanics trying to make big bucks on the unsuspecting consumer. Not that this doesn't happen in little shops in town that have been in buisness a long time either. Who ever needs thier lowerballjoint on an 88 replaced feel free to email me and i will help you replace it FREE!!!!!!!!(if your near southern pa/northern maryland/west virginia.) <IMG SRC="/ubb/redface.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>)
